# The project video, as a cue sheet.
#
# Every frame is the real games being played by the real autopilot, rendered
# through the real cell shader -- this is the plugin running, not a mock-up.
# Render with:
#
#   ./build/coinopgl --sequence /tmp/co-frames --script tools/video.cues \
#                    --size 1920x1080 --seconds 46 --fps 30
#
# Syntax: `T Name=V` sets at a time, `T1..T2 Name=V1..V2` ramps between two
# (eased, because a parameter that starts and stops abruptly reads as a cut).
#
# Values are the 0..1 the host sees, except Game, Palette, Fit and Aspect, which
# are indices -- the same split the plugin itself has, see Controls.h.
#
# The order of the piece is the order of the argument: the game everybody knows
# first, then the one that does something to your footage, then the two that are
# best on a wall, then the one that should not fit in a grid and does. The grid
# goes coarse at the end, because pixel mapping is the thing worth staying for.

# ------------------------------------------------------- 0s: Snake, Phosphor
# Green CRT, because that is what this is. Nothing clever for the first eight
# seconds -- a snake, playing itself, in a plugin.
0.0   Game=0
0.0   Palette=0
0.0   Speed=0.40
0.0   Skill=0.85
0.0   Grid=0.415
0.0   Round=0.25
0.0   Gap=0.18
0.0   Glow=0.40
0.0   React=0.35

# The glow comes up as the snake gets longer, so the picture builds rather than
# sitting still.
1.0..8.0   Glow=0.40..0.62

# ------------------------------------------------------- 8s: Bricks, Amber
# The one that earns the effect plugin. Amber because the brick rows read as
# rows in it.
8.0   Game=1
8.0   Palette=1
8.0   Speed=0.50
8.0   Skill=0.80
8.0   Difficulty=0.55
8.0   Glow=0.45
8.0   Round=0.10
8.0   Gap=0.22

# Round the cells off through the middle of the section -- the same playfield,
# a different object.
11.0..16.0  Round=0.10..0.85

# ------------------------------------------------------ 17s: Marchers, Ice
17.0  Game=2
17.0  Palette=2
17.0  Speed=0.55
17.0  Skill=0.75
17.0  Difficulty=0.5
17.0  Round=0.30
17.0  Gap=0.16
17.0  Glow=0.50

# The formation accelerates as it thins out -- that is the game, not the cue
# sheet. All this does is lift the reactive glow so the last few read as urgent.
20.0..26.0  React=0.35..0.85

# ------------------------------------------------------ 26s: Rally, Candy
# Three moving objects on a black field. The most legible thing here at any
# size, which is why it is the one just before the grid goes coarse.
26.0  Game=3
26.0  Palette=3
26.0  Speed=0.55
26.0  Skill=0.72
26.0  Round=0.45
26.0  Gap=0.20
26.0  Glow=0.55
26.0  React=0.45

# --------------------------------------------------------- 34s: Drift, Fire
# The vector game, rasterised. Rocks are rotating polygons Bresenham'd into the
# same cells everything else uses.
34.0  Game=4
34.0  Palette=5
34.0  Speed=0.5
34.0  Skill=0.8
34.0  Difficulty=0.55
34.0  Round=0.20
34.0  Gap=0.14
34.0  Glow=0.55

# ------------------------------------------------- 39s: the pixel-map ending
# Drop the grid to nineteen cells across and open the gaps. This is what the
# plugin looks like when the composition is a fixture layout and Resolume's
# pixel mapper is sampling it -- and it is the reason even the vector game is
# drawn as cells. A hairline vector would not survive this.
39.0..44.0  Grid=0.415..0.25
39.0..44.0  Gap=0.14..0.42
39.0..44.0  Round=0.20..1.00
39.0..44.0  Glow=0.55..0.80
